  LIMIT: this run grades the RULE and any admission record an adopter serves. Nothing here observes a sensor running or a person; no sensing substrate exists in this estate.
  LIMIT: the refusals it can emit are: covert-sensing, duplicate-key, field-not-admissible, key-not-declared, kind-not-admissible, ladder-could-not-walk, names-or-identifies-an-individual, no-dpia-record, not-this-scenario-class, required-key-missing, role-not-registered, scenario-not-served, sensor-not-named, value-not-the-declared-shape. That list is read off this module's own call sites, so a refusal the table declares and no call site spells is refused at load; it does not prove every one of them is reachable.
  LIMIT: served bytes are parsed with a loader that refuses a DUPLICATE mapping key, so the document this check grades is the document a reader sees, not the one PyYAML builds by keeping the last of two identical keys.
  LIMIT: the three scanned directories -- people, scenarios and sensor-admissions -- read both .yaml and .yml, and anything else under them is a named row. The DPIA path pattern accepts only .yaml, so a DPIA served under the short spelling is refused as missing: fail-closed, and named here because the asymmetry is real.
  LIMIT: the identifier scan covers FIVE served documents and no others: the admission record, the DPIA record it names and every role file in the people register are scanned for identifier-shaped KEYS AND VALUES; every bus-factor-key-person scenario file and every readable party.yaml, whatever roles it claims, are scanned for identifier-shaped VALUES only, because their shape is not closed and `note:` is a legitimate key there. Within those it refuses a key the table does not declare, a mapping or list under a declared key, a slot whose type or fixed value the table declares, a required or non-empty key that is absent, an identifier-SHAPED filename, id, key or value, an email address and a UK national insurance number.
  LIMIT: no rule here reads English, so within those documents a personal name written in plain words survives in a DPIA's lawful_basis; a DPIA's what_is_sensed; a DPIA's what_is_not_sensed; a notice's published_at path; a role id or a role file's own role: prose; any other prose in a served scenario file or a party.yaml, and is refused by nothing.
